<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</h1><p class="st-meta">1899 CE – 1956 CE · capital Khartoum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sudan under a joint British and Egyptian condominium from 1899, in practice a British colony, until independence in 1956.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Kitchener</a>'s reconquest destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-condominium">Condominium Agreement</a> in January 1899. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> was formally shared, but a British governor-general, appointed on British advice, held supreme authority.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>British officials governed through native administration, using tribal chiefs and sultans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira-scheme">Gezira Scheme</a>, opened in 1925, made irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> the main export. The south was administered separately under a Southern Policy that excluded northern influence and favoured Christian missions. After the assassination of governor-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-stack">Lee Stack</a> in 1924, Egyptian troops were expelled.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>An Anglo-Egyptian agreement in 1953 provided for self-government and self-determination. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> became independent on 1 January 1956, but the separate treatment of the south contributed to a civil war that had already begun in 1955.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/anglo-egyptian-sudan.jpg" alt="Anglo-Egyptian Sudan" width="320" height="526"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_sudan_under_british_control.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1899 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khartoum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Herbert Kitchener, Reginald Wingate</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (north); traditional religions and Christianity (south)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a href="/countries/south-sudan">South Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Condominium of Sudan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=anglo-egyptian-sudan" aria-label="Anglo-Egyptian Sudan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5568 1597 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Anglo-Egyptian Sudan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5888 1853L5695 1926M5888 1853L5615 1933M5888 1853L5886 1850M5888 1853L4997 651M5888 1853L5811 1337M5888 1853L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5695 1926h0M5615 1933h0M5886 1850h0M4997 651h0M5811 1337h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5888 1853h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5888 1853h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khartoum. <a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=anglo-egyptian-sudan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
